WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:02.967
A Storm Called Katrina On Sunday, August 28, 2005, twelve thousand people huddled inside the Superdome

2
00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:13.884
in New Orleans, Louisiana. Thousands more waited outside. Built to fit about seventy thousand people, the

3
00:00:13.885 --> 00:00:22.775
Superdome was a huge sports arena where professional football games and concerts were held. But on that

4
00:00:22.776 --> 00:00:30.861
day, people were not there for a game. Public officials had just named the stadium an

5
00:00:30.862 --> 00:00:40.532
emergency shelter. The building was open to New Orleans residents who were unable to evacuate the

6
00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:46.111
city as a huge storm—a dangerous hurricane called Katrina—approached. The next morning Katrina arrived, pelting the

7
00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:51.011
Superdome with violent winds and rain. Soon the building's generators failed, leaving everyone in darkness. The wind

8
00:00:51.012 --> 00:01:00.410
tore a huge hole in the ceiling, and food shortages, sweltering heat, and backed-up toilets added

9
00:01:00.411 --> 00:01:08.321
to the chaos inside the arena. As hours passed people grew more and more frustrated. There

10
00:01:08.322 --> 00:01:12.234
were reports of violence and crime. Outside the building the situation was just as grim. By

11
00:01:12.235 --> 00:01:21.493
August 30 the worst of the storm was over, but nearly 15 feet of floodwater remained,

12
00:01:21.494 --> 00:01:27.645
submerging 80 percent of the city. Even worse, over a thousand people had drowned, and now corpses

13
00:01:27.646 --> 00:01:35.134
floated in the streets. It would take years for New Orleans to recover from Katrina, one

14
00:01:35.135 --> 00:01:37.142
of the strongest hurricanes ever to form in the Atlantic Ocean. Hurricane Havoc Hurricanes are powerful

15
00:01:37.143 --> 00:01:41.467
storms that start over warm oceans. As their winds spin and move across the water, they

16
00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:46.456
gain heat and energy. If these hurricanes reach land, they can cause serious damage with their strong

17
00:01:46.457 --> 00:01:53.892
winds, heavy rain, and high waves. Scientists measure a hurricane's intensity by using the Saffir-Simpson Hurricane

18
00:01:53.893 --> 00:02:02.446
scale, which divides hurricanes into five categories. Category One is the weakest, and Category Five is

19
00:02:02.447 --> 00:02:08.104
the strongest. Hurricane Katrina began near The Bahamas in the Caribbean Sea on August 23, 2005.

20
00:02:08.105 --> 00:02:14.778
The winds and relentless rain grew stronger as they traveled westward. Two days later the storm reached

21
00:02:14.779 --> 00:02:19.117
the southern tip of Florida as a Category One hurricane, damaging homes and property and causing

22
00:02:19.118 --> 00:02:28.764
widespread flooding. Four people were killed. By the time Katrina reached the Gulf of Mexico, it

23
00:02:28.765 --> 00:02:38.907
had become a huge storm, with winds over 130 miles per hour. By August 28 Katrina

24
00:02:38.908 --> 00:02:48.005
had grown into a Category Five hurricane—and this time the city of New Orleans lay right

25
00:02:48.006 --> 00:02:50.243
in its path. Trouble in the Big Easy In 2005 no place was more vulnerable to this

26
00:02:50.244 --> 00:02:56.244
monster storm than New Orleans. This city, affectionately known as the Big Easy, is the birthplace

27
00:02:56.245 --> 00:03:05.475
of jazz music and home of a famous celebration called Mardi Gras. It is also a

28
00:03:05.476 --> 00:03:15.559
city surrounded by water, lying within the Mississippi River basin, with the Gulf of Mexico to

29
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.976
the south and the giant Lake Pontchartrain to the north. Levees, or man-made walls, protected the city

30
00:03:19.977 --> 00:03:25.132
from flooding. But in 2005 some of these levees were old and in need of rebuilding.

31
00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:33.951
Heavy rain and high floodwaters were predicted with Katrina, and experts were deeply concerned that these

32
00:03:33.952 --> 00:03:41.874
levees would not be able to hold back the water. As the storm came closer, government

33
00:03:41.875 --> 00:03:49.786
officials issued evacuation orders, requiring residents to leave many parts of Louisiana. Hundreds of thousands of

34
00:03:49.787 --> 00:03:55.363
people evacuated the coastal region, heading for other states or to shelters away from the coast. In

35
00:03:55.364 --> 00:04:04.029
New Orleans buses shuttled some residents to emergency shelters such as the Superdome. But many people

36
00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:08.583
stubbornly refused to leave their homes; they were determined to wait out the storm, no matter

37
00:04:08.584 --> 00:04:14.589
how many warnings were issued. On the morning of August 29, Katrina finally hit New Orleans.

38
00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:20.351
Experts had downgraded the hurricane to Category Three, but it was still a powerful storm. Driven by

39
00:04:20.352 --> 00:04:24.835
strong winds, great walls of ocean water crashed along the coastline. As experts had predicted, the

40
00:04:24.836 --> 00:04:30.506
city's system of levees failed. Water from the river and lake poured into the streets, flooding

41
00:04:30.507 --> 00:04:35.069
New Orleans from all sides. People who had chosen not to evacuate were suddenly in terrible

42
00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:40.244
danger. Some tried to leave their homes, only to be swept away by rushing waters. Others

43
00:04:40.245 --> 00:04:45.876
headed for attics or rooftops, where they ended up stranded for days. Nearly 1,500 of the city's

44
00:04:45.877 --> 00:04:50.985
residents died as a result of the hurricane. Many more grew ill from contaminated floodwater, and

45
00:04:50.986 --> 00:04:58.794
cases of dysentery, cholera, and typhoid fever sprang up. Thousands suffered mental-health problems due to the

46
00:04:58.795 --> 00:05:05.039
stress of losing their homes and family members. Louisiana was hit the hardest, but Katrina struck

47
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.877
seven states in total. By the time it was over, the storm had killed over 1,800 people

48
00:05:09.878 --> 00:05:19.943
and caused billions of dollars in damage, leaving hundreds of thousands of people without homes. One

49
00:05:19.944 --> 00:05:26.704
government official called Katrina the "worst catastrophe, or set of catastrophes, in the country's history." Help

50
00:05:26.705 --> 00:05:28.704
Pours In During the hurricane and immediately afterward, only the military and police were allowed to

51
00:05:28.705 --> 00:05:35.610
help Katrina's victims. Tens of thousands of soldiers were flown to the scene to rescue stranded

52
00:05:35.611 --> 00:05:44.333
and injured people, and deliver supplies like food and clean drinking water. Later civilian volunteers from all

53
00:05:44.334 --> 00:05:49.425
over the world arrived to help too. The American government, under President George W. Bush, decided

54
00:05:49.426 --> 00:05:57.261
to contribute $60 billion to the disaster relief. The Red Cross, as well as groups such

55
00:05:57.262 --> 00:06:08.997
as the Salvation Army and Habitat for Humanity, also raised money for the victims and led

56
00:06:08.998 --> 00:06:14.416
efforts to supply clothing, food, and temporary shelter. People outside the city helped by housing the homeless

57
00:06:14.417 --> 00:06:19.993
and donating money and supplies. Businesses pitched in too, with employees collecting contributions and preparing meals.

58
00:06:19.994 --> 00:06:21.993
A Healing Process The Hurricane Katrina disaster has not been erased from the minds and daily

59
00:06:21.994 --> 00:06:28.245
lives of its victims. Yet tremendous strides have been made in rebuilding the places hit hardest,

60
00:06:28.246 --> 00:06:35.229
most notably New Orleans. New homes, schools, and businesses have been constructed, along with better levees to

61
00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:42.205
protect the city from flooding. Weather experts are improving ways to predict the intensity of hurricanes,

62
00:06:42.206 --> 00:06:54.217
while emergency management experts are working to create better systems for alerting—and evacuating—people in danger. The

63
00:06:54.218 --> 00:06:58.032
Superdome in New Orleans offers yet another sign of hope. After Katrina many residents wanted the

64
00:06:58.033 --> 00:07:05.706
damaged stadium to be demolished because of all the unpleasant memories that surrounded it. Yet a

65
00:07:05.707 --> 00:07:15.279
group of people, including its manager, Doug Thornton, rallied to save it, arguing that people still associated

66
00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:17.367
many happy memories with the Superdome. Their efforts succeeded. Just one year after Katrina, the building

67
00:07:17.368 --> 00:07:26.336
was completely restored, and the city's professional football team, the Saints, returned there to play. "We

68
00:07:26.337 --> 00:07:34.186
did it," manager Doug Thornton said of the amazing feat. "We had to do it. We

69
00:07:34.187 --> 00:07:37.577
couldn't fail." Just as amazing was the Saints' record that year. With the Superdome completely filled with

70
00:07:37.578 --> 00:07:46.323
excited fans, the team won its first home game and went on to have one of

71
00:07:46.324 --> 00:07:50.912
its best seasons ever. And that gave the residents of New Orleans a reason to celebrate.

